The employer must give at least 60 days' written notice to end an indefinite-term contract of a worker paid monthly. The basis is Article 75 of the Saudi Labor Law, as amended by Royal Decree M/44 (1446H), which took effect around 18–19 February 2025; that amendment also cut the worker's notice to 30 days, and both sides give 30 days if pay is not monthly. I checked this only against law-firm summaries, not the official Ministry of Human Resources text, and the Al Tamimi and STA Law Firm pages linked below agree on the 60 days.

Sources: [Al Tamimi – Key amendments](https://www.tamimi.com/news/key-amendments-to-saudi-arabias-labour-law/), [STA Law Firm](https://www.stalawfirm.com/en/blogs/view/key-amendments-to-saudi-arabias-labour-law), [Etqan – Article 75](https://etqanlawfirm-sa.com/en/?p=22763)

