Female workers get **12 weeks** of maternity leave on full pay under Article 151 of the Saudi Labor Law, as amended by Royal Decree M/44 (1445H) and in force since February 2025; it used to be 10 weeks. Six of those weeks must be taken right after the birth, the rest can be used flexibly, and the leave can start up to four weeks before the expected delivery date. I checked this against law-firm and press summaries rather than the official gazette text, and those sources give slightly different effective dates in February 2025.

Sources: [Gulf News](https://gulfnews.com/world/gulf/saudi/saudi-arabia-maternity-leave-increased-to-12-weeks-from-10-1.103757932), [Clyde & Co](https://www.clydeco.com/en/insights/2025/04/KSA-Labour-Law-amendments-leave-entitlement), [Morgan Lewis](https://morganlewis.com/pubs/2024/08/key-amendments-to-the-kingdom-of-saudi-arabia-labour-law-announced), [King & Spalding](https://www.kslaw.com/Insights/Articles/amendments-to-the-saudi-labor-law-approved)

